<Project>
  <!--
    Joinery.Dovetail.ModularPipelines.targets
    Declares the ModularPipelines host model for a project that references this package directly: a DovetailHost item, which
    Joinery.Dovetail.Hosts.targets expands into ConfigureDovetail extension methods on ModularPipelines' PipelineBuilder. They
    apply the project's joints to the builder and build the pipeline through DovetailPipelineHelpers.Configure, which
    Joinery.Dovetail.Hosting (a dependency of this package) emits into the project from DovetailPipelineHelpers.cs.template.

    Only an assembly that imports joints (ImportDovetailAssembly, which the SDK sets for applications and test projects) gets the entry
    points: they call DovetailContextBuilder.Create(), which only such an assembly has. A library that references the package can still
    export PipelineBuilder joints.

    Joinery.Dovetail.Sdk declares the same host itself (Joinery.Dovetail.HostDetection.targets), before NuGet imports this file, so the
    host is declared once. NuGet imports this file for a package reference; the repository's own projects that use the host (the
    sample and the tests) import it by path. DovetailModularPipelinesHost=false leaves the host out, for a project that wires Dovetail in
    through DovetailPipelinePlugin or DovetailPipelineHelpers itself.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(ImportDovetailAssembly)' == 'true' and '$(DovetailModularPipelinesHost)' != 'false' and @(DovetailHost->AnyHaveMetadataValue('Identity', 'ModularPipelines')) != 'true'">
    <DovetailHost
      Include="ModularPipelines"
      HostBuilderType="global::ModularPipelines.PipelineBuilder"
      HostBuilderName="builder"
      HostType="global::ModularPipelines.IPipeline"
      HelperName="global::Joinery.Dovetail.DovetailPipelineHelpers"
      HasBuildExpression="false" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
